"""Phase 4 copy, batch A: heart rate zones, max heart rate, zone 2, interval walking. Original prose (ADR 0036)."""
from phase4_common import base, emit, s

D = {}

MEASURE_REST = s("Measuring resting heart rate",
    "Resting heart rate is easiest to measure in the morning, before you get out of bed and before coffee. Lie still for a minute, then count your pulse at the wrist or neck for 30 seconds and double it, or read it from a chest strap or watch. Repeat on three or four mornings and use the average, because a single reading can move by five beats or more with sleep, stress, alcohol or a hard session the day before.",
    "Most adults sit between 60 and 80 beats per minute at rest. Endurance athletes often sit in the 40s or low 50s. A resting rate that rises by several beats above your usual for a few days in a row can be a sign of poor recovery, illness or stress, and is a reasonable cue to take an easier day.")

D["heart-rate-zone-calculator"] = base(
    "heart-rate-zone-calculator",
    title="Heart rate zone calculator",
    h1="Heart rate zone calculator",
    description="Work out your five heart rate training zones from your age, by percentage of max heart rate or with the Karvonen heart rate reserve method.",
    intro="Enter your age to see five training zones in beats per minute, based on an estimated maximum heart rate. Adding your resting heart rate moves the zones onto heart rate reserve (Karvonen), which fits them more closely to your fitness.",
    q="How do I calculate my heart rate zones?",
    a="Estimate your maximum heart rate, then take a percentage of it for each zone. With Tanaka's formula (208 − 0.7 × age), a 30-year-old's max is 187 bpm. Zone 2, at 60 to 70%, is 112 to 131 bpm. With a resting rate of 60, the Karvonen method moves zone 2 to 136 to 149 bpm.",
    sections=[
        s("What heart rate zones are",
          "Heart rate zones divide the range between easy and maximal effort into bands, each linked with a different kind of training. Low zones build the aerobic base and help recovery. Middle zones develop stamina at a steady, sustainable pace. High zones push the lactate threshold and the top end of aerobic capacity. Training across the zones, in the right proportions, develops all of these qualities.",
          "Zones are useful because heart rate is an objective, real-time signal of effort. Pace changes with hills, wind, heat and fatigue; heart rate reflects how hard your body is working regardless of the conditions. A zone gives a client a clear target on an easy day, when the temptation is to go too fast, and on a hard day, when they need to know they have reached the intended effort."),
        s("The five zones",
          ("table", ["Zone", "% of max HR", "Feels like", "Used for"], [["Z1 Recovery", "50 to 60%", "Very easy, relaxed breathing", "Warm-ups, cool-downs, recovery days"], ["Z2 Aerobic", "60 to 70%", "Easy, can hold a conversation", "Aerobic base, long steady sessions"], ["Z3 Tempo", "70 to 80%", "Comfortably hard, short sentences", "Stamina and steady-state work"], ["Z4 Threshold", "80 to 90%", "Hard, a few words at a time", "Lactate threshold, race-pace intervals"], ["Z5 VO2 max", "90 to 100%", "Very hard, can't talk", "Short intervals for top-end fitness"]]),
          "These percentages are one common scheme. Some coaching systems use three zones, others seven, and the boundaries differ. What matters is choosing one scheme and using it consistently, so that a zone 2 session means the same thing every week."),
        s("Estimating maximum heart rate",
          "Every zone depends on maximum heart rate, so the estimate matters. The calculator uses Tanaka's formula, 208 − 0.7 × age, published in 2001 from a meta-analysis of 351 studies and later confirmed in a laboratory study. It also shows the older 220 − age rule for comparison. For a 30-year-old, Tanaka gives 187 bpm and 220 − age gives 190.",
          "Both formulas describe averages. Real maximum heart rates vary widely between people of the same age, with a typical spread of 10 to 12 beats either way and some people 20 beats away. If your zones feel wrong in practice, for example if zone 2 feels like hard work or zone 4 feels easy, your true max is probably different from the estimate. A field test, done carefully, gives a better number."),
        s("Percentage of max versus Karvonen",
          "The simplest method multiplies maximum heart rate by each zone's percentage. It works without any other information, but it ignores fitness. Two people with the same max but very different resting heart rates get the same zones, even though their easy and hard efforts sit at different heart rates.",
          "The Karvonen method, published by Martti Karvonen in 1957, uses heart rate reserve: maximum heart rate minus resting heart rate. Each zone is the resting rate plus a percentage of the reserve. For a 30-year-old with a max of 187 and a resting rate of 60, the reserve is 127 beats. Zone 2 at 60 to 70% becomes 60 + 76 to 60 + 89, or 136 to 149 bpm, compared with 112 to 131 bpm on the simple method.",
          "Karvonen zones sit higher, especially in the lower zones, and tend to match how the effort feels for fitter people with low resting heart rates. For beginners, the simple percentage method often feels more comfortable in the easy zones. Pick the method that best matches your perceived effort, and keep using it."),
        MEASURE_REST,
        s("How to train with zones",
          "Most endurance coaches favour a polarised or pyramid distribution: a large share of training, often around 75 to 80%, in the easy zones 1 and 2, and a smaller share in zones 4 and 5. Zone 3 is used for some steady work but is easy to overuse, because it feels productive while being too hard to recover from quickly and not hard enough to drive top-end gains.",
          ("ul", [
              "**Easy days:** keep heart rate in zone 1 or 2 for the whole session, even if it means walking hills.",
              "**Long sessions:** mostly zone 2, with heart rate allowed to drift a few beats late in the session.",
              "**Threshold sessions:** intervals of 5 to 20 minutes in zone 4, with easy recovery between.",
              "**VO2 max sessions:** short intervals of 2 to 5 minutes in zone 5, with full recovery.",
          ]),
          "For general fitness, two or three zone 2 sessions a week plus one harder session is a sound starting point."),
        s("Why heart rate drifts and lags",
          "Heart rate takes a minute or two to catch up with a change in effort, so short intervals may finish before heart rate reaches the target zone. For intervals under two minutes, use pace or perceived effort instead.",
          "During long sessions, heart rate tends to rise slowly even at a steady pace, especially in heat or when dehydrated. This cardiac drift is normal. Heat, caffeine, stress, poor sleep and illness can all raise heart rate for the same effort, which is why a session can feel easier or harder than the number suggests. Use heart rate together with how the effort feels, not as the only guide."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What are the five heart rate zones?", "Zone 1 recovery (50 to 60% of max), zone 2 aerobic (60 to 70%), zone 3 tempo (70 to 80%), zone 4 threshold (80 to 90%) and zone 5 VO2 max (90 to 100%)."),
        ("Which max heart rate formula does this calculator use?", "Tanaka, 208 − 0.7 × age, with the classic 220 − age shown for comparison. Both are averages and can be 10 to 20 bpm away from your real max."),
        ("Should I use percentage of max or Karvonen?", "Karvonen uses your resting heart rate and often fits fitter people better. The simple method needs only age. Choose the one that matches how the efforts feel."),
        ("How much time should I spend in each zone?", "Many coaches suggest around three quarters of training in zones 1 and 2, with the rest in harder zones. Zone 3 is useful but easy to overdo."),
        ("Why does my heart rate go up during a steady run?", "Cardiac drift: heart rate rises slowly over long sessions, especially in heat or when dehydrated, even at the same pace."),
        ("Are wrist heart rate monitors accurate enough?", "For steady sessions they are usually close. For intervals and fast changes, a chest strap is more accurate."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Max heart rate calculator", "/tools/max-heart-rate-calculator"), ("Zone 2 heart rate calculator", "/tools/zone-2-heart-rate-calculator"), ("VO2 max calculator", "/tools/vo2-max-calculator")],
)

D["max-heart-rate-calculator"] = base(
    "max-heart-rate-calculator",
    title="Max heart rate calculator",
    h1="Max heart rate calculator",
    description="Estimate maximum heart rate by age with the 220 − age, Tanaka and Gellish formulas. Add a resting rate to see your heart rate reserve.",
    intro="Enter your age to compare three maximum heart rate formulas side by side. Add a resting heart rate to see your heart rate reserve, and get zone 2 and threshold ranges to start training with.",
    q="How do I calculate my maximum heart rate?",
    a=["Use an age-based formula. The classic rule is 220 − age; Tanaka is 208 − 0.7 × age; Gellish is 207 − 0.7 × age. For a 30-year-old these give 190, 187 and 186 bpm. For a 50-year-old they give 170, 173 and 172 bpm.",
       "These are averages. Your real maximum may be 10 or more beats away, and only a hard field test or lab test measures it."],
    sections=[
        s("What maximum heart rate is",
          "Your maximum heart rate is the ceiling: the fastest your heart will beat in a minute when you are working flat out. It is set largely by genetics and age, and it falls gradually through adult life, by roughly seven beats a decade. Training does not raise it in any meaningful way. A fit 50-year-old and an unfit 50-year-old may have similar maximum heart rates; what differs is how much work they can do before they reach it.",
          "Maximum heart rate is not a measure of fitness. A high max does not mean someone is fitter, and a low one does not mean they are less fit. It is a reference point that defines the top of the heart rate range, so that training zones can be set as fractions of it. That is why a reasonable estimate matters, even though the number itself says little about performance."),
        s("The three formulas",
          ("table", ["Formula", "Equation", "Origin"], [["Fox (classic)", "220 − age", "A 1971 rule of thumb from a summary of earlier studies"], ["Tanaka", "208 − 0.7 × age", "2001 meta-analysis of 351 studies and 18,712 people, confirmed in the lab"], ["Gellish", "207 − 0.7 × age", "2007 long-term study of the same adults tested repeatedly"]]),
          "220 − age is the best known but the least precise. It was never derived from a formal study, and it tends to overestimate max heart rate in younger adults and underestimate it in older adults. Tanaka and Gellish come from much larger, more careful data sets and give very similar results, within a beat or two of each other.",
          "For a 20-year-old, the formulas give 200, 194 and 193 bpm. For a 60-year-old, they give 160, 166 and 165 bpm. The gap between the classic rule and the newer formulas grows with age, which matters most for older clients whose zones would otherwise be set too low."),
        s("How far off can a formula be?",
          "Age explains only part of the variation in maximum heart rate. In the studies behind these formulas, the standard deviation is about 10 to 12 beats. In practical terms, roughly two thirds of people of a given age have a max within 10 to 12 beats of the formula, and about one in twenty sits more than 20 beats away.",
          "That spread is large enough to matter. If a 40-year-old's true max is 195 bpm rather than the 180 that 220 − age suggests, every zone set from the formula will be about 10 beats too low. Easy runs will feel very easy and threshold sessions will feel like tempo runs. The reverse happens for someone whose true max is below the formula.",
          "If a formula-based zone feels wrong over several sessions, trust the feeling and consider a field test."),
        s("Testing your maximum heart rate",
          "A field test can find a better number, but it is demanding. Only do it if you are healthy, already training regularly and have no heart, lung or blood pressure concerns. If you are unsure, speak to a doctor first.",
          ("ol", [
              "Warm up for 15 minutes with easy running or cycling, building to a few short, faster efforts.",
              "Run or ride hard for three minutes, at an effort you could only just hold for that long.",
              "Recover easily for two minutes.",
              "Repeat the three-minute effort, building to an all-out finish over the last 30 seconds.",
              "The highest heart rate recorded in the final minute is close to your maximum.",
              "Cool down for at least 10 minutes.",
          ]),
          "A hill makes it easier to reach a true max without very high speed. A chest strap records the peak more accurately than a wrist monitor. Do the test fresh, after an easy day, and expect the number to be a few beats under your absolute max; most people stop short of it."),
        s("Heart rate reserve",
          "Heart rate reserve is the gap between your maximum and resting heart rates. If your estimated max is 187 and your resting rate is 60, your reserve is 127 beats. The Karvonen method sets training zones as a share of this reserve, added back to the resting rate, which tends to match how efforts feel better than a share of max alone.",
          "Reserve does change with fitness, because resting heart rate falls as aerobic fitness improves. A fall in resting heart rate from 70 to 60 over a few months of training adds ten beats to the reserve and is one of the simplest signs that fitness is improving."),
        MEASURE_REST,
        s("Using max heart rate in training",
          "Once you have a max, the next step is training zones. The calculator shows two of the most used: zone 2, at 60 to 70% of max, for aerobic base work, and zone 4, at 80 to 90%, for threshold sessions. For a 30-year-old using Tanaka, those are 112 to 131 and 150 to 168 bpm. The heart rate zone calculator gives all five.",
          "Recalculate every few years as you age, or sooner if you get a tested max. Keep using the same formula or test result so that your zones stay consistent from one block of training to the next."),
        s("Factors that change heart rate on the day",
          ("ul", [
              "**Heat and humidity:** raise heart rate for the same effort, sometimes by 10 beats or more.",
              "**Dehydration:** reduces blood volume, so the heart beats faster to compensate.",
              "**Caffeine and stimulants:** can raise heart rate at rest and during exercise.",
              "**Altitude:** raises heart rate at submaximal effort in the first days at height.",
              "**Fatigue, stress and illness:** can push heart rate up or, after heavy training, blunt it.",
              "**Medication:** beta blockers and some other drugs lower heart rate and make formula zones unreliable.",
          ]),
          "If you take medication that affects heart rate, ask your doctor how to judge exercise intensity; perceived effort is often a better guide."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What is a normal maximum heart rate?", "It depends mainly on age. Tanaka's formula gives about 187 bpm at 30, 173 at 50 and 166 at 60, with individuals commonly 10 to 12 beats either side."),
        ("Is 220 minus age accurate?", "It is a rough guide. It tends to overestimate max in young adults and underestimate it in older adults. Tanaka and Gellish are based on better data."),
        ("Can training increase my maximum heart rate?", "Not meaningfully. Training lowers resting heart rate and increases how much work you can do, but max is set mainly by genetics and age."),
        ("Is a high maximum heart rate good?", "Not in itself. A high or low max is neither better nor worse; it is a reference point for setting zones, not a fitness measure."),
        ("How do I find my real max heart rate?", "With a hard field test after a thorough warm-up, or a lab test. Only test if you are healthy and already training, and speak to a doctor if unsure."),
        ("Why does my watch show a higher max than the formula?", "Your true max may be higher than average for your age, or the watch may have recorded a brief error. Check against a chest strap if you can."),
        ("Does max heart rate differ for men and women?", "Only slightly. Some formulas suggest small sex differences, but age explains far more of the variation."),
        ("What if I take beta blockers?", "They lower heart rate, so age-based formulas and zones will be too high. Use perceived effort and ask your doctor for guidance."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Heart rate zone calculator", "/tools/heart-rate-zone-calculator"), ("Zone 2 heart rate calculator", "/tools/zone-2-heart-rate-calculator"), ("VO2 max calculator", "/tools/vo2-max-calculator")],
    disclaimer="General information only, not medical advice. Maximal exercise tests are not suitable for everyone. Speak to a doctor before hard exercise if you have a heart condition, high blood pressure, take medication that affects heart rate, or are unsure.",
)

D["zone-2-heart-rate-calculator"] = base(
    "zone-2-heart-rate-calculator",
    title="Zone 2 heart rate calculator",
    h1="Zone 2 heart rate calculator",
    description="Find your zone 2 heart rate range by % of max, the Karvonen heart rate reserve method or the MAF 180 formula, with a talk test to check it.",
    intro="Enter your age and resting heart rate to find your zone 2 range. Compare three methods, percentage of max, Karvonen and MAF, and add a tested maximum if you have one.",
    q="How do I calculate my zone 2 heart rate?",
    a="The most common method is 60 to 70% of maximum heart rate, with max estimated as 220 − age. For a 40-year-old that is 180 × 0.6 to 0.7, or 108 to 126 bpm. Karvonen, using a resting rate of 60, gives 132 to 144 bpm. The MAF method gives a ceiling of 180 − 40 = 140 bpm, with a range of 130 to 140.",
    sections=[
        s("What zone 2 is",
          "Zone 2 is steady, easy aerobic exercise: hard enough to raise your breathing and heart rate, easy enough to keep going for a long time while holding a conversation. It is the effort of a relaxed jog, a brisk walk uphill or an easy ride where you could chat with a partner. In laboratory terms, it sits around or just below the first lactate threshold, the point where lactate in the blood starts to rise above resting levels.",
          "At this intensity the body relies mainly on fat and aerobic metabolism to produce energy. Training here builds the systems that support all endurance performance: more mitochondria in muscle cells, more capillaries delivering blood, a stronger heart and better use of fat as fuel. These adaptations take time, which is why zone 2 work is done in longer, frequent sessions."),
        s("Three ways to calculate it",
          ("table", ["Method", "Formula", "Age 40, resting 60"], [["% of max HR", "60 to 70% of (220 − age)", "108 to 126 bpm"], ["Karvonen", "Resting + 60 to 70% of (max − resting)", "132 to 144 bpm"], ["MAF", "180 − age as the ceiling, 10 beats below as the floor", "130 to 140 bpm"]]),
          "The percentage-of-max method is simple but ignores fitness. Karvonen adds the resting heart rate, which lifts the range for fitter people with low resting rates. Phil Maffetone's MAF method takes 180 minus age as an aerobic ceiling, with adjustments he recommends for illness, injury, beginners and very consistent athletes. The calculator uses the basic MAF figure.",
          "The methods disagree by design: they come from different coaching traditions and different definitions of zone 2. None is definitively right. Use the talk test to choose the one that matches your experience."),
        s("The talk test",
          "The simplest check of zone 2 is whether you can talk. At the right effort, you can speak in full sentences, though you would notice your breathing. If you can only manage a few words at a time, you are above zone 2. If you could sing, you are probably below it.",
          "Research comparing the talk test with laboratory measures has found it a reliable marker of the first ventilatory threshold, which sits close to the top of zone 2. That makes it a valuable cross-check when a formula gives a number that feels too easy or too hard. Over time, many people learn to recognise zone 2 effort without looking at a watch."),
        s("Why zone 2 matters",
          "For endurance athletes, zone 2 builds the aerobic base that supports faster work. Elite runners, cyclists and rowers often spend 75 to 80% of their training time at low intensity. That volume of easy work allows them to recover and absorb the harder sessions that make up the rest.",
          "For general health, regular moderate aerobic exercise is linked with lower risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and early death. Zone 2 sessions fit within the widely recommended 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity a week. They are also easier to recover from than hard sessions, so they can be done more often, and they are accessible to people of most fitness levels."),
        s("How much zone 2 to do",
          "A common starting point is two to four sessions a week of 30 to 60 minutes, building towards longer sessions over months. Endurance athletes may do much more, with sessions of two hours or longer. The key is consistency: zone 2 adaptations build over weeks and months of regular work.",
          ("ul", [
              "**Beginners:** two or three sessions of 20 to 30 minutes, walking if needed to stay in zone.",
              "**Regular exercisers:** three or four sessions of 40 to 60 minutes, plus one harder session.",
              "**Endurance athletes:** most weekly volume in zone 2, with one or two hard sessions.",
          ]),
          "Walking uphill, cycling, rowing, swimming and elliptical training all work. Choose activities that let you hold a steady effort."),
        MEASURE_REST,
        s("Common zone 2 mistakes",
          ("ul", [
              "**Going too hard.** Zone 2 often feels too easy at first, and many people drift into zone 3.",
              "**Ignoring drift.** Heart rate rises slowly during long sessions; slow down to stay in range.",
              "**Using short intervals.** Zone 2 benefits come from sustained, continuous effort.",
              "**Judging by pace.** Pace in zone 2 varies with heat, hills and fatigue. Follow heart rate and the talk test.",
              "**Expecting fast results.** Aerobic adaptations take weeks to show.",
          ]),
          "For many runners, staying in zone 2 means running more slowly than they expect, or walking hills, at least at first. Over months, the pace at the same heart rate usually improves, which is one of the clearest signs that aerobic fitness is growing."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What is my zone 2 heart rate?", "Commonly 60 to 70% of maximum heart rate. For a 40-year-old using 220 − age, that is 108 to 126 bpm. Karvonen and MAF give higher ranges."),
        ("Which zone 2 method is best?", "None is definitively right. Use the one that matches the talk test: in zone 2 you can speak in full sentences."),
        ("Why is my zone 2 pace so slow?", "Aerobic fitness takes time to build. Many people start slower than they expect; pace at the same heart rate improves over months."),
        ("How long should zone 2 sessions be?", "Usually 30 to 60 minutes for general fitness, building to longer sessions for endurance goals."),
        ("Is zone 2 good for fat loss?", "It burns a high share of fat during the session and is easy to recover from, so it supports fat loss alongside diet. Total energy balance matters most."),
        ("What is the MAF method?", "Phil Maffetone's approach uses 180 − age as an aerobic ceiling, with adjustments for health and training history."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Heart rate zone calculator", "/tools/heart-rate-zone-calculator"), ("Max heart rate calculator", "/tools/max-heart-rate-calculator"), ("Interval walking calculator", "/tools/interval-walking-calculator")],
)

D["interval-walking-calculator"] = base(
    "interval-walking-calculator",
    title="Interval walking calculator",
    h1="Interval walking calculator",
    description="Calculate calories for Japanese interval walking or a custom brisk and easy walking session, and compare it with a steady walk of the same length.",
    intro="Plan a Japanese walking session, three minutes brisk and three minutes easy repeated five times, or set your own intervals. See calories for each part, a steady-walk comparison and your weekly total.",
    q="How many calories does Japanese interval walking burn?",
    a="At 175 lb (79 kg), a 30-minute session of five rounds of three minutes fast (4.0 mph) and three minutes comfortable (2.5 mph) burns about 167 calories: 104 in the brisk intervals and 63 in the easy ones. A steady 3 mph walk for the same 30 minutes burns about 146. Four sessions a week add up to about 667 calories.",
    sections=[
        s("What Japanese interval walking is",
          "Interval walking training, often called Japanese walking, alternates three minutes of fast walking with three minutes of slow walking, repeated for about 30 minutes. It was developed by Hiroshi Nose and colleagues at Shinshu University in Matsumoto, Japan, as a practical way for middle-aged and older adults to improve fitness without a gym.",
          "In the original protocol, the fast intervals are at about 70% of each person's peak aerobic capacity, a pace that feels somewhat hard, and the slow intervals at about 40%, a comfortable stroll. Participants aim for at least five sets per session and at least four days a week. The simplicity is the point: it needs only a watch and a place to walk."),
        s("What the research found",
          "In a five-month study published in 2007, Nose's team compared interval walking with continuous moderate walking in adults aged around 60. The interval group improved their aerobic capacity and thigh muscle strength more, and saw larger falls in blood pressure. The continuous walking group, despite walking for similar time, saw smaller changes.",
          "Later studies in Japan and elsewhere, including people with type 2 diabetes, have generally supported these benefits, particularly for blood pressure, blood sugar control and fitness. The method has been used with thousands of participants in community programmes in Matsumoto. Its main advantage is not extra calories but a stronger training effect for the same time spent walking."),
        s("How the calculator works",
          "The calculator uses MET values, multiples of resting energy use, from the Compendium of Physical Activities, a widely used reference that lists energy costs for hundreds of activities. Calories per minute are MET × 3.5 × body weight in kg ÷ 200.",
          ("table", ["Walking pace", "MET"], [["Stroll, 2.0 mph (3.2 km/h)", "2.8"], ["Comfortable, 2.5 mph (4.0 km/h)", "3.0"], ["Steady, 3.0 mph (4.8 km/h)", "3.5"], ["Brisk, 3.5 mph (5.6 km/h)", "4.3"], ["Fast, 4.0 mph (6.4 km/h)", "5.0"]]),
          "It multiplies each pace's calories per minute by the time spent at that pace, adds the two, and compares the total with a steady 3 mph walk for the same duration. The weekly figure multiplies the session total by the number of sessions."),
        s("Worked example",
          "Take someone weighing 175 lb, or 79.4 kg. At 4.0 mph, 5.0 METs gives 5.0 × 3.5 × 79.4 ÷ 200 = 6.9 calories a minute. Fifteen minutes of fast intervals is about 104 calories. At 2.5 mph, 3.0 METs gives 4.2 calories a minute, so fifteen minutes of easy intervals is about 63 calories. The session total is about 167 calories.",
          "A steady walk at 3.0 mph for 30 minutes, at 3.5 METs, burns about 146 calories. The interval session burns about 21 more, roughly 15%. Over four sessions a week, the interval sessions total about 667 calories.",
          "Choosing the brisk 3.5 mph option and a 2.0 mph stroll lowers the session to about 148 calories, barely more than steady walking. The fast intervals are where the extra effort, and most of the training benefit, comes from."),
        s("How fast is fast?",
          "In the research, fast intervals were set at about 70% of each person's measured peak aerobic capacity. Without a lab test, use effort: the fast intervals should feel somewhat hard, around 6 or 7 on a 10-point scale. Breathing becomes noticeably heavier, and you can speak only in short phrases. The slow intervals should feel easy, around 3 out of 10.",
          "For many people, fast means 4 mph or a little more, a pace close to the transition to jogging. Older or less fit walkers may reach the right effort at 3.5 mph, or on a slight incline. Arms swinging and a slightly shorter, quicker stride help increase speed without strain."),
        s("Fitting it into a week",
          ("ul", [
              "**Start with three sessions a week** of three to five rounds, then build to four or more sessions of five rounds.",
              "**Warm up** with a few minutes of easy walking before the first fast interval.",
              "**Use a timer** with a beep at each change, so you don't need to watch the clock.",
              "**Choose a flat, safe route** at first; hills can be added later for extra challenge.",
              "**Combine with strength training** twice a week for the broadest health benefits.",
          ]),
          "Japanese walking suits people who find running hard on the joints, are returning to exercise, or want a structured walk with a clear goal. It also works well as a recovery or easy day for people who run or lift."),
        s("Calories in context",
          "A difference of 20 to 30 calories a session is small. Over a week of four sessions, it adds up to around 100 calories, about the energy in a banana. Interval walking is best seen as a way to improve fitness and blood pressure efficiently, not as a major calorie burner.",
          "For weight loss, overall eating and total daily activity matter far more than the choice between interval and steady walking. A daily step target, consistent strength training and a moderate calorie deficit will move the scale more than any change of walking pattern."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What is Japanese interval walking?", "Three minutes of fast walking alternated with three minutes of slow walking, repeated about five times, at least four days a week. It was developed at Shinshu University in Japan."),
        ("Does interval walking burn more calories than steady walking?", "A little. For a 175 lb person, a 30-minute session burns about 167 calories, compared with about 146 for a steady 3 mph walk."),
        ("How fast should the fast intervals be?", "Somewhat hard, around 6 or 7 out of 10 in effort. For many people that is about 4 mph, close to the pace where walking turns into jogging."),
        ("What are the main benefits?", "In the research, bigger improvements in aerobic fitness, leg strength and blood pressure than steady walking for the same time."),
        ("How often should I do it?", "The original protocol aims for at least four sessions a week of five rounds. Start with three sessions and build up."),
        ("How accurate are the calorie numbers?", "They are MET-based estimates. Individual calorie use varies with fitness, body composition, terrain and walking technique."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Zone 2 heart rate calculator", "/tools/zone-2-heart-rate-calculator"), ("Rucking calorie calculator", "/tools/rucking-calorie-calculator"), ("Steps to calories calculator", "/tools/steps-to-calories-calculator")],
)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    from phase4_a_extra import EXTRA

